<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konavle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Konavle is a fertile region and municipality at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavtat">Cavtat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. The Republic of Ragusa bought it from Bosnian nobles in two stages, in 1419 and 1427, gaining its main source of grain and wine. It is known for its traditional costume and embroidery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konavle.jpg" alt="Konavle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Modzzak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavtat,_Croatia.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Konavle is a municipality and small Dalmatian subregion southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, in Dubrovnik-Neretva County. It is a narrow field between the Sniježnica mountain and the Adriatic Sea, running from the coastal town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavtat">Cavtat</a> to the Montenegrin border at Prevlaka. Only Cavtat, the largest settlement, and the southernmost village, Molunat, lie on the coast; the other 30 villages are inland. Sniježnica, at 1,234 metres, is the highest point of the county. The municipality had 8,577 inhabitants in 2011, of whom 97.1 per cent were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, and its historical inhabitants were called Canalesi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. In the 5th and 4th centuries BCE the Illyrian tribes of the Pleraei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardiaei">Ardiaei</a> settled there, and in the 2nd century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> developed a trade and maritime centre at the site of the Greek colony of Epidaurus. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> canalis and refers to irrigation canals that brought water from the spring at Vodovađa to Epidaurus. Remains include a Roman aqueduct, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villa-estate">villae</a> rusticae and a Mithraic monument at Močići. In the Middle Ages Konavle was part of Travunia, at various times a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Serbian, Dioclean and Bosnian rulers, and it has necropolises of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomil">Bogomil</a> stećak tombstones.</p><p>At the start of the 15th century powerful Bosnian lords held Konavle: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandalj-hranic-kosaca">Sandalj Hranić</a> in the east and the brothers Petar and Radoslav Pavlović in the west. The Republic of Ragusa bought the eastern part from Sandalj on 24 June 1419 and the western part, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavtat">Cavtat</a> and Obod, from Radoslav Pavlović a few years later. Ragusa made it a feudal estate governed by a count seated at Pridvorje, elected by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> for six months and later a year. A First Konavle War followed from 1430 to 1433. In 1427 Ragusa set out to eradicate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomilism</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, converting the whole population.</p><p>Konavle remained part of the Republic of Ragusa, as its largest granary, until the republic was abolished in 1808. After a short French occupation it was ravaged by Montenegrin and Russian invaders, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 it passed to Austrian rule. A railway opened there in 1901. In the Croatian War of Independence the region was occupied by Yugoslav Army forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> from October 1991 to May 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>'s airport lies near the Konavle village of Čilipi. Remains from the Ragusan period include the Count's Palace in Pridvorje and the Soko Tower at Dunave. The painter Vlaho Bukovac and the jurist Baltazar Bogišić come from Konavle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Konavle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
